{% extends 'base.en.html.twig' %}
{% set nav_actif = 'apropos' %}
{% set route_fr = 'paleo_apropos' %}
{% set route_de = 'paleo_de_apropos' %}

{% block titre %}About the authors{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}Marie-Noëlle and Joël-Marie Kreilos, author and contributor to this blog: musical palaeography, medieval-music teaching, and the technical work behind Paléo.{% endblock %}

{% block contenu %}
<h1>About the authors</h1>
<p class="chapeau">
    <strong>Marie-Noëlle Kreilos</strong> — musician, teacher, and the author of this
    blog. <em>Paléo</em> grew out of her own path, between the classroom and the
    study room.
</p>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Where the name “Paléo” comes from</span>
    <p>
        Marie-Noëlle Kreilos taught music in Schaffhausen (Switzerland) and in
        Germany. She founded the small medieval-music ensemble <em>Hexacordum</em>.
        For several years, this ensemble gave workshops and courses on medieval
        music in Alsace, Germany and Switzerland. Holding a postgraduate diploma in
        musicology from the <strong>University of Strasbourg</strong>, Marie-Noëlle
        Kreilos studied <strong>musical palaeography</strong>&nbsp;: the discipline
        that deciphers old musical writing — the very one that gave this blog its
        name. That taste for neumes and old manuscripts never left her&nbsp;:
        <em>Paléo</em> is its continuation, meant to pass it on simply.
    </p>
</div>

<p class="lettrine">
    One foot in music teaching, the other in the study of old manuscripts&nbsp;: this
    dual background gives <em>Paléo</em> its particular tone — that of a teacher used
    to making technical knowledge accessible, applied here to a thousand-year-old
    script.
</p>

<p class="chapeau">
    <strong>Joël-Marie Kreilos</strong>, musician, musicologist and computer
    scientist. He holds a First Prize in baroque flute from the conservatoire
    and a bachelor's degree in musicology from the Sorbonne.
    He handled the technical side of this blog and contributed a
    critical reading of the text.
</p>

<div class="encadre">
    <span class="etiquette">A word about this blog</span>
    <p>
        <em>Paléo</em> was born from the wish to share a script as beautiful as it is
        little-known, without jargon or prerequisites. A remark, a question&nbsp;? The
        suggestion form at the bottom of the <a href="{{ path('paleo_en_glossaire') }}">glossary</a>
        is open.
    </p>
</div>
{% endblock %}
